5	// Installed by the runner through a generated `--settings` file, in exec form:
6	//   { "type": "command", "command": <node>, "args": [<this file>, <policy.json>],
7	//     "timeout": 30, "onFailure": "block" }
8	//
9	// Hook protocol (code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks, verified for Claude Code 2.1.314):
10	//   stdin   one JSON object: session_id, cwd, hook_event_name, tool_name, tool_input, tool_use_id, ...
11	//   deny    print {"hookSpecificOutput":{"hookEventName":"PreToolUse","permissionDecision":"deny",
12	//           "permissionDecisionReason":"..."}} and exit 0 (the reason is shown to Claude)
13	//   pass    print nothing and exit 0 (no opinion: the normal permission rules decide)
14	//   error   exit 2 with a message on stderr: blocks the call. `onFailure: "block"` additionally makes
15	//           a crash, a timeout or unparsable output block the call, so the guard fails closed.
16	//
17	// What a hook can and cannot see. For Read / Edit / Write the hook sees the one file the call
18	// touches. For Grep and Glob it sees only the search root and the pattern text, while the tool
19	// walks the whole tree below the root: in Claude Code 2.1.314 Grep runs ripgrep with `--hidden`
20	// (only VCS folders excluded) and Glob runs `rg --files --hidden --no-ignore`, and a whitelist
21	// glob such as `**/*` matches dot-names too (ripgrep globs follow .gitignore rules, where `*`
22	// matches a leading '.'). No pattern check can therefore keep hidden entries out of a search.
23	// Instead the guard scans the search root before the call: Grep and Glob are allowed only on a
24	// folder that contains no hidden entry at any depth (symlinks are not followed, matching ripgrep,
25	// which does not follow them either). Pattern checks remain for patterns that spell out hidden
26	// names or try to leave the root.
27	//
28	// The decision logic is exported (`decide`) so the runner's TypeScript code and tests use exactly
29	// the code that the hook runs. This file deliberately has no dependencies beyond Node built-ins.
